Troubleshooting: DC01 Recurring BSOD — Windows Defender Kernel Driver Conflict
Root Cause

Event log analysis revealed MpKslDrv.sys — Windows Defender's kernel-level scanning driver — was being loaded each time Defender updated its definitions. In a VMware VM environment this driver conflicts with VMware's own kernel drivers, triggering IRQL_NOT_LESS_OR_EQUAL crashes. The timestamp of the driver installation matched precisely with BSOD occurrences confirming it as the cause.

Source: BugCheck
Code: 0x0000000a (IRQL_NOT_LESS_OR_EQUAL)
Parameters: (0x0000000000000001, 0x00000000000000ff, 0x0000000000000000, 0xfffff804776bb190)
IRQL at crash: 0xff (255) — impossibly high, confirms kernel driver conflict not resource pressure
Minidump: C:\Windows\Minidump\081026-15171-01.dmp
Diagnostic Commands Used
powershell
# Pulled last 20 system errors
Get-EventLog -LogName System -EntryType Error -Newest 20 | Format-List TimeGenerated, Source, Message > C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\errors.txt

# Listed recently installed drivers
Get-WinEvent -LogName System | Where-Object {$_.Id -eq 7045} | Select-Object TimeCreated, Message | Format-List > C:\Users\Administrator\Desktop\drivers.txt

# Listed minidump files
Get-ChildItem C:\Windows\Minidump | Select-Object Name, LastWriteTime | Sort-Object LastWriteTime -Descending
Fix Applied

Windows Defender disabled via Group Policy — PowerShell commands were blocked by Defender's self-protection mechanism and denied access even from an Administrator account.

gpedit.msc → Computer Configuration → Administrative Templates → Windows Components → Microsoft Defender Antivirus:

Turn off Microsoft Defender Antivirus → Enabled

Microsoft Defender Antivirus → Real-time Protection:

Turn off real-time protection → Enabled
Turn off behavior monitoring → Enabled
Turn off process scanning whenever real-time protection is enabled → Enabled
powershell
# Forced policy to apply immediately
gpupdate /force

Restarted DC01 to fully unload Defender's kernel drivers from memory.

Why This Is Acceptable in a Lab

DC01 is an isolated VM on a private labnet segment with no internet access. There is nothing for Defender to protect against in this environment. In a production environment Windows Defender would remain enabled and the fix would involve configuring exclusions for VMware processes rather than disabling Defender entirely.

