Lessons Learned
An IRQL value of 0xff (255) in a BugCheck parameter indicates a kernel driver calling at an invalid interrupt level — this points to a driver conflict, not resource pressure
Minidump files in C:\Windows\Minidump contain detailed crash data — analyzing them alongside the driver installation log (Event ID 7045) can pinpoint the exact cause of recurring BSODs
Windows Defender protects itself from being stopped via PowerShell even by Administrator accounts — Group Policy is the correct override path
Timestamp correlation between driver installation events and crash events is a reliable diagnostic technique — if a driver loaded at 6:05 PM and a BSOD hit at 6:21 PM, that driver is the first suspect
In isolated lab VMs with no internet exposure, disabling Defender is a reasonable stability fix — in production, configure exclusions instead
